-- functions.spl — LLM function definitions for Recipe 89: arXiv Digest (EAAI-27 workshop)
-- Imported by summarize_arxiv_paper.spl via IMPORT 'functions.spl'.
--
-- Two-step map-reduce per paper, matching the EAAI-27 workshop capstone spec
-- (docs/conference/EAAI-2027/.../handout.md, "Capstone Demo: arXiv Paper
-- Summarization"): one summary per section (map), then one structured
-- per-paper digest — a paragraph per section plus a 3-bullet contribution
-- summary (reduce).

CREATE FUNCTION section_summarizer(section_title TEXT, section_text TEXT, max_tokens INT)
RETURNS TEXT
AS $$
You are a technical summarizer. Produce a concise summary of the following
research paper section in plain English. Focus on methods and findings.
Stay well under {max_tokens} words — leave headroom, since generation is cut
off at a hard token limit and an unfinished sentence is worse than a shorter
complete one. Write 2-4 complete sentences and stop; do not pad toward the
limit. Write plain prose only — no headings, no bullet points, no preamble
such as "This section...".

Section: {section_title}

{section_text}
$$;

CREATE FUNCTION group_summarizer(group_summaries TEXT, max_tokens INT)
RETURNS TEXT
AS $$
You are a research editor. Below are section-by-section summaries from one
research paper. Condense them into a single Markdown block for an intermediate
reduction step. Start with a "### " header on its own line — use the first
section title found in the input as the label. Then write a dense paragraph
of 3-5 complete sentences covering the key methods and findings across all
input sections. No bullet points, no preamble.
Stay well under {max_tokens} words — leave headroom, generation is cut off at
a hard token limit; an unfinished sentence is worse than a shorter complete one.

Section summaries:
{group_summaries}
$$;

CREATE FUNCTION paper_digest_writer(title TEXT, section_summaries TEXT, max_tokens INT)
RETURNS TEXT
AS $$
You are a research editor producing a structured digest of one paper for a
live audience unfamiliar with it. You are given per-section summaries below,
each already condensed. Using ONLY the information in these summaries (do
not invent details not present in them), write the following two parts IN
THIS EXACT ORDER:

1. A "**Key Contributions:**" block with exactly 3 bullet points capturing
   the paper's core contribution, method, and main result. Write this FIRST
   so it is always complete even if the token budget runs out mid-digest.

2. One paragraph per section, in the same order as given, each introduced by
   the section's name as a bold label (e.g. "**Introduction:** ...").
   Sentence budget per paragraph scales with section count:
   - 1-6 sections  → up to 4 sentences each
   - 7-12 sections → up to 2 sentences each
   - 13+ sections  → 1 sentence each, strictly

Hard length budget: {max_tokens} tokens total; generation is cut off with no
warning. Key Contributions comes first so the most important block always
finishes. After writing it, divide the remaining budget evenly across
sections — stop cleanly at any section boundary rather than truncating
mid-sentence inside one.
Write Markdown.

Paper title: {title}

Section summaries:
{section_summaries}
$$;
